From 870ac02579b07e4056f70545927f9922211d03e7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Jolyon Brown <jolyon@limilo.com>
Date: Thu, 12 Nov 2026 19:50:14 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 17/28] Fix exponential walk and stack overflow in structure
 copy forwarding check

CheckSingleUseGlobalLoadStruct (can a load be forwarded to the uses of
the local it was stored in) is guarded by mPatched, but since 1073206
(Fix too strong check for native structure copy forwarding) a block
first reached behind an overwrite of the local is walked a second time,
as poisoned, when a path reaches it without an overwrite, and nothing
recorded that this had been done: every such path walked the block and
everything behind it again. With a run of N independent conditionals
behind the block that is 2^N walks, so the compile time tripled with
every conditional added. With a loop behind it that does not store to
the local, the second walk came back to the block it started from,
started again, and overflowed the stack: the compiler crashed on
element_loop() of the new test.

The second walk is now done once per block (mPatchRecheck). An arrival
at a block whose second walk is still running fails as before: such a
block has mPatchUsed set, and a second walk that completed has left it
clear, since a poisoned walk fails at the first use it finds. The
mPatchFail term added to that test is defensive: every arrival inside a
second walk is poisoned or cleared, so the old condition already fails
there. Repeating the second walk had one more effect, which is kept:
started again from a block inside it, it could pass a block that
overwrites the local and arrive, cleared, back at the block whose
second walk was running, and fail there. This happens when an
overwriting block leads straight back into a block that is walked a
second time after it, so the overwriting block marks its successors
(mPatchRejoin) and a marked block searches the blocks behind it for the
overwriting block (CheckSingleUseGlobalLoadStructLoop). The check gives
the same answer as before wherever the old one came back; where it
overflowed the stack the answer is now false, which leaves the store in
place.

The test has a function with 14 conditionals behind the overwrite, the
loop that overflowed the stack, and four more shapes of the check with
asserts on the values. The functions are called with more than one
argument value so that the arguments are not folded into them.

This is the part of the change proposed for upstream main (Fix
exponential walks and a stack overflow in native code block checks)
that the two preceding commits make necessary.

Co-Authored-By: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
